le is at the higher temperature? <b>Justify</b> your answer using the graph.","points":1,"rubric":[{"point":"Y, because its distribution is shifted to higher speeds (peak farther right, broader), so its average kinetic energy is greater.","accept":["Y; higher speeds mean higher average kinetic energy"]}],"sample":"Sample Y. Its curve peaks at a higher speed and is spread toward faster speeds, so its atoms have a greater average kinetic energy, which means a higher temperature."},{"label":"b","prompt":"Which sample exerts the greater pressure? <b>Explain</b> in terms of collisions of the atoms with the walls.","points":1,"rubric":[{"point":"Y, because its faster atoms collide with the walls more often and with greater force (same n and V).","accept":["Y; more frequent and harder collisions"]}],"sample":"Sample Y. With the same number of atoms in the same volume, its faster atoms hit the walls more often and each collision pushes harder, so the pressure is greater."},{"label":"c","prompt":"A sample of neon (20.18 g/mol) is at the same temperature as sample X. <b>Compare</b> the average kinetic energy of the neon atoms with that of the argon atoms in X, and <b>calculate</b> the ratio of their average speeds, v(Ne) : v(Ar).","points":2,"rubric":[{"point":"States the average kinetic energies are equal because the temperatures are equal.","accept":["same average kinetic energy"]},{"point":"Calculates v(Ne)/v(Ar) = √(39.95/20.18) = 1.41.","accept":["1.41","square root of the mass ratio"]}],"sample":"Equal: average kinetic energy depends only on temperature. From ½m(Ne)v(Ne)² = ½m(Ar)v(Ar)², v(Ne)/v(Ar) = √(39.95/20.18) = √1.980 = 1.41, so neon atoms move about 1.41 times as fast."}]}